#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294120 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e016cedd-0206-555c-8934-c6d19566d547" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Area Between Two Curves as an Integral by First Solving For the Limits of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d32a5dd5-a34f-5f2d-831e-7317b1b6e73b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{3/4}(-{x}^{2}+2x-6)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294122} {:answer/id #uuid "e7a7e746-e6b3-50bb-b723-4f710817d632" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1/4}^{3/4}(8{x}^{2}+x-3)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294123} {:answer/id #uuid "7ba6270e-1c65-5526-8e14-b85b0b7c39aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{3/4}^{1}(-8{x}^{2}-3x+2)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294124} {:answer/id #uuid "3dcb7b46-232a-586c-af00-748069ef18ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{3/4}(-4{x}^{2}-x+4)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294125} {:answer/id #uuid "fcdf8ae2-e353-5d35-97e1-4d825481885c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{3/4}(-8{x}^{2}-2x+6)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294126}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fcdf8ae2-e353-5d35-97e1-4d825481885c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{3/4}(-8{x}^{2}-2x+6)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294126} :answer-field/id #uuid "290f832c-5316-5e4e-8a45-7de3f3173b83" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294121}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1495ffd4-708e-5569-a19d-b4ce8001edba" :question/math-academy-id "q-121517" :question/problem "Which integral gives the area of the finite region bounded between $y=8{x}^{2}-4$ and $y=2-2x?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a sketch of the two functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033713/assets/b2f34492ce184169d0e720d5a2214a356236b56d6e20bd8ece584b967f074e64.png)\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-2x & =8{x}^{2}-4 \\\\ 8{x}^{2}+2x-6 & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{-2\\pm \\sqrt{{2}^{2}-4\\cdot 8\\cdot (-6)}}{2\\cdot 8} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2\\pm 14}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-1$ to $x=\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033713/assets/94243d27b465eca7957731385b2fe13fae36b811079d3077e2e093aa19d81ad3.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=8{x}^{2}-4,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=2-2x.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{3/4}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{3/4}[\\,(2-2x)-(8{x}^{2}-4)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{3/4}(-8{x}^{2}-2x+6)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294127 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e016cedd-0206-555c-8934-c6d19566d547" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Area Between Two Curves as an Integral by First Solving For the Limits of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42551b9f-abde-5f3c-8eb6-5688a1c996a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{5/4}(-4{x}^{2}-x+5)\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294129} {:answer/id #uuid "c63ecd13-53f8-5eb9-b484-f05af70dae96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{5/4}(-4{x}^{2}+x+5)\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294130} {:answer/id #uuid "fa07377b-3bd3-5e51-bab3-af0a96068486" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{4/5}(4{x}^{2}-x+5)\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294131} {:answer/id #uuid "75594be6-5839-5706-9bdf-2a9cf4607bf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{5/4}(4{x}^{2}-x-5)\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294132} {:answer/id #uuid "73516eb3-9dc5-5773-819c-7d52696e0ae5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-5/4}^{1}(4{x}^{2}+x-5)\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294133}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c63ecd13-53f8-5eb9-b484-f05af70dae96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{5/4}(-4{x}^{2}+x+5)\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294130} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0f4d607-ef7b-5645-a072-8c0f866eed83" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294128}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a08163fa-4d1f-55bf-a571-4e4e9630646e" :question/math-academy-id "q-121566" :question/problem "Which integral gives the area of the finite region bounded by $y=2{x}^{2}-3x$ and $y=5-2{x}^{2}-2x?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a sketch of the two functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033713/assets/9a4de823003f0461231c355a43ba9845358ec4a3767ca59c2ba2206456620373.png)\n\n*(Note: Figure not drawn to scale)*\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-3x & =5-2{x}^{2}-2x \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-x-5 & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1\\pm \\sqrt{(-1)^{2}-4\\cdot 4\\cdot (-5)}}{2\\cdot 4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\pm 9}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-1$ to $x=\\frac{5}{4}.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033713/assets/94b0113f7ca9ffeca11398cce06995fa4a7e5cb3f39b3021d5d6ec56746b75ee.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=2{x}^{2}-3x,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=5-2{x}^{2}-2x.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{5/4}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{5/4}[\\,(5-2{x}^{2}-2x)-(2{x}^{2}-3x)\\,]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{5/4}(-4{x}^{2}+x+5)\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294134 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8fa1bebf-4af0-5a5c-962c-8a88f8392776" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Area Between Two Curves Expressed as Functions of X"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6df5d658-8a88-569f-a6b5-e8ca18e71cb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294136} {:answer/id #uuid "761624e2-5716-5636-b33d-fb9a4df2696c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294137} {:answer/id #uuid "5213550d-4fb9-5591-980a-92aa2ae603f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294138} {:answer/id #uuid "610f1d4a-aa4e-576b-85e8-e09d78fda35b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294139} {:answer/id #uuid "2d309c77-9608-5ba3-8956-9c2d3f5cb945" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{62}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294140}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6df5d658-8a88-569f-a6b5-e8ca18e71cb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294136} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e745bd6-4ba1-5bfc-a396-75968cb361a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294135}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c84d87bd-9d44-56d4-92d0-f67ba8ac76ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-121585" :question/problem "Calculate the area of the finite region bounded by the curves $y=2{x}^{2}-2$ and $y=6.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a sketch of the two functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033713/assets/0fd8a89bdcad9f1b20c9fd35df9aa209a5e551d5d7a7c97f62103e23e7ae9741.png)\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-2 & =6 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-2$ to $x=2.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, we draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033713/assets/83e8d6af5284006b4caad6b1a96cb1298f7c7672d6c35fa652359d3a0b28e037.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=2{x}^{2}-2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=6.$\n\nTherefore, we can find the area, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}[\\,(6)-(2{x}^{2}-2)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}(8-2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(8x-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3})|}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =[8\\cdot 2-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {2}^{3}]-[8\\cdot (-2)-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (-2)^{3}] \\\\  & =(16-\\frac{16}{3})-(-16+\\frac{16}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294141 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8fa1bebf-4af0-5a5c-962c-8a88f8392776" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Area Between Two Curves Expressed as Functions of X"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0b13a58-949d-59bd-b81d-b7146f916083" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294143} {:answer/id #uuid "dcfa1c93-7144-5efd-a7f6-dd4226d855db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294144} {:answer/id #uuid "9bb367bd-abc7-594b-ae48-30d1fd779359" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294145} {:answer/id #uuid "a076330d-e49e-53cf-a0b7-93b2b2c02acb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{256}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294146} {:answer/id #uuid "9e1ea365-e568-5919-b405-f3c90be18740" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{251}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294147}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a076330d-e49e-53cf-a0b7-93b2b2c02acb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{256}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294146} :answer-field/id #uuid "03e42d4f-1366-595e-be2b-243c9be99de4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294142}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c2412cf4-7cbd-5fc0-a3ce-3581348a7006" :question/math-academy-id "q-121772" :question/problem "Calculate the area of the finite region bounded by the curves $y={x}^{2}-10$ and $y=6.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a sketch of the two functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033713/assets/9248483d8b55c2ac61fcc3c1f11fee0eabee92794eaacd1fb2e37fff68cec289.png)\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-10 & =6 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =16 \\\\ x & =\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-4$ to $x=4.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, we draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033713/assets/173d726e4b0ecee37d8b7ca8878f5bc5934a069dc48a2b9d18e28d72f3b04f7e.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y={x}^{2}-10,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=6.$\n\nTherefore, we can find the area, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-4}^{4}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{4}[\\,(6)-({x}^{2}-10)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{4}(16-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(16x-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3})|}_{-4}^{4} \\\\  & =[16\\cdot 4-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {4}^{3}]-[16\\cdot (-4)-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-4)^{3}] \\\\  & =(64-\\frac{64}{3})-(-64+\\frac{64}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{256}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294148 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8fa1bebf-4af0-5a5c-962c-8a88f8392776" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Area Between Two Curves Expressed as Functions of X"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29ee47f7-9e46-528f-bf70-34a3947a8bb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294150}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29ee47f7-9e46-528f-bf70-34a3947a8bb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294150} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2245c1f-04cb-53e9-b142-66a2689d6ad3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294149}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dabd07a4-7ceb-5d01-9a56-3f969a97f96a" :question/math-academy-id "q-356029" :question/problem "Calculate the area of the finite region bounded by the curves $y={x}^{2}-3$ and $y=1.$\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a sketch of the two functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033713/assets/664fcc791ec42b4ec40ed3fc7033fa601d646c9900632864122d31fd01a2cf61.png)\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we equate the two functions and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-3 & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=-2$ to $x=2.$\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, we draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033713/assets/1d10b1994774239767578417bc5f0b74a6a7369005a73fbac859574c9d5d36e0.png)\n\nThe vertical line \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y={x}^{2}-3,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=1.$\n\nTherefore, we can find the area, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}[\\,(1)-({x}^{2}-3)\\,]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}(4-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(4x-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3})|}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =[4\\cdot 2-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {2}^{3}]-[4\\cdot (-2)-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-2)^{3}] \\\\  & =(8-\\frac{8}{3})-(-8+\\frac{8}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}-(-\\frac{16}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
